Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 06:25:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09276/11362] Bound generated engines, fix an O(n^2) explorer
 regression, recover a lost sweep

Four verified pieces from one session. Each was re-run and confirmed here, not
accepted from a report. Committed with --no-verify under explicit operator
authorization; see the last section for what that skipped and what was run by
hand instead.

1. ENGINE-CALL GUARD (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6400)

A generated sb26 engine called `flood(r, c, val, visited=None)` without `visited`
at both click branches. Any click on a component of two or more same-valued cells
re-pushed forever. The process hit ~78 GB RSS, exhausted 147 GB of swap, and
earlyoom killed it -- twice, same seed.

The harness ran generated code in-process with no bound on one call's time or
memory; `max_nodes` bounds how MANY calls a search makes, never the cost of one.
The scored agent shares that path.

signal.alarm cannot fix it -- the eval runs one thread per game and Python
delivers signals only on the main thread, so a signal watchdog would look like a
guard and protect nothing. The guard raises into the offending thread via
PyThreadState_SetAsyncExc, re-fires per poll so generated code's broad `except`
cannot swallow it, and clears deterministically in `finally`.

Wired at BOTH live sites: `WorldModelVerifier.score` runs the same engine
per-transition upstream of plan_in_model, so guarding only the planner would have
left the earlier exposure open. Uninterruptible C-level calls and process-wide
RSS attribution are NOT covered; both fail toward a skipped candidate. 9 tests.

2. INCREMENTAL BUDGET-EXHAUSTION ESTIMATOR

`BUDGET_AWARE_SEARCH_ENABLED` went default-on 2026-08-09 (ba2bd569eb). Its
estimator was called per action over the whole ever-growing frame buffer and
rescanned all history: O(n^2). Profiled on m0r0 it was 76.8 s of 98.6 s -- 77% of
the run -- and 6,184,719 signature computations.

That is what blocked today's submission gate. Games were hitting the 115 s
subprocess cap and being scored unsolved. They were never coverage failures:
m0r0 still solves L1 at 4x BASELINE efficiency, and cd82 now solves one level
DEEPER than baseline. The gate killed them during a post-solve tail.

Fixed by folding the estimator incrementally: each frame's signature computed
once at append. Equivalence is BIT-IDENTICAL, not approximate -- plain `==` on
full result dicts, verified step-for-step over 420 steps, at the real call site,
and in vivo: post-fix m0r0 prints the byte-identical trajectory line to pre-fix
(actions=3507 eff=0.0008 nav_reset=1114 nav_fwhr=0.5804) at 126 s -> 16 s.
Estimator share 77% -> 0.8%. Per-call cost now flat in history (ratio 1.07).
101 tests.

Two things this does NOT fix, both pre-existing and proven separate by a
same-seed A/B against a shim reproducing the old semantics: a residual
superlinear per-action cost on deep full-budget runs (cd82 unlucky seeds, ft09,
ls20 -- all non-core), and lp85's efficiency against a stale committed baseline,
which is the operator-accepted 2026-08-07 frontier trade awaiting an
--update-baseline that was deliberately not run.

3. RECALL-GATED RESAMPLE -- default OFF, and deliberately NOT the design proposed

The proposed gate was "resample when fitted-window recall < 0.99". That design was
rejected with evidence: recall is scored on the SAME window the prompt showed, and
tr87's two ~1.0 cells hardcode 33 and 38 window coordinates while its 0.354 cell
does not. A 0.99 gate re-rolls a non-memorizer until a memorizer appears --
selection FOR memorization, at exactly the near-ceiling margin where it lives. It
would have improved the metric and worsened hidden games. mem_scan is no rescue
either: tu93's 0.0 cell IS a memorizer and both its 1.0 cells are not.

What shipped fires only when the downstream trust gate has ALREADY rejected the
draw, so it can never rank or discard an engine the pipeline would use -- the
defense is structural, not hopeful. Catastrophic-only (recall < 0.6), requires
n_changing >= 3 because recall is 0.0 by definition on a quiet window, refuses
under a pinned seed (a re-call would buy a byte-identical answer), and restores
the engine-store file byte-identically when the original is kept.

Honest position from its author: safe to have, NOT yet safe to flip. Each firing
is a full generation, roughly +8-23% induction time against a saturated budget.
Enabling it needs a matched-budget A/B. 18 tests; inert by default, verified.

4. RECOVERED SWEEP + STOPPED-RUN EVIDENCE

results/arc_live_oracle_gap.json was restored to the real 2026-07-19 measurement
(25 rows, live 4, oracle 183) in the previous commit; this adds the stopped
head-to-head evidence: 15 cells, 6 preserved engines including the sb26 one.

The head-to-head compares NOTHING -- it was stopped before the gemma arm ran, so
it is not a model result. It is kept for its diagnostics, which drove everything
above. Its cells do show single-shot induction variance: failures and successes
on the SAME game at different seeds, one generation per cell (the retry ladder
never fired), which matters because the live agent gets one attempt per game.

WHAT --no-verify SKIPPED

Only one hook actually blocks: artifact-freshness-lint. It ALREADY FAILS on clean
HEAD with 5+ stale artifacts drifted against arc_competition_agent.py and
arc_executable_world_model.py from earlier commits -- verified in a detached
worktree at HEAD. It is a pre-existing backlog, not a consequence of this work,
and clearing it honestly means rebuilding artifacts or filing per-dependency
inert-drift acknowledgements, which is separate work.

Run by hand instead, all clean: ruff check and ruff format on all 11 changed
python files, determination-preservation-lint, canonical-url-lint, and every test
suite named above.
